WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.970
The first season of Netflix's long-awaited
adaptation of The Witcher books remains remarkably

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.880
faithful to the spirit of the novels.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.671
In fact, it's packed with small details that
only those well-versed in the ways of the

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.360
Continent will notice.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.200
The Witcher isn't the first live-action adaptation
of the beloved novels.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:21.250
In 2002, actor Michal Zebrowski played Geralt
in the Polish TV series The Hexer, which was

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.960
also edited into a critically panned feature
film.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.300
Like Netflix's The Witcher, The Hexer adapted
stories from the first two Witcher books,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.650
The Last Wish and Sword of Destiny, while
mixing in some original elements.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.520
Zebrowski doesn't cameo in The Witcher, but
that hasn't stopped him from playing Geralt

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.640
again…sort of.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.680
While it's still Henry Cavill's handsome mug
that they'll see on-screen, Polish fans should

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.650
find Geralt's voice very familiar.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.950
Zebrowski is providing Geralt's dialogue in
The Witcher's Polish-language dub, reprising

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.860
the role he played almost 20 years ago.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.320
It's not a detail that most English-speaking
viewers will notice, but it's a nice treat

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.940
for the Polish fans in the audience.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.329
The Witcher isn't the only Geralt-adjacent
project Zebrowski has in the pipeline, either.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:08.700
The actor will also dub Keanu Reeves' character
in the upcoming video game Cyberpunk 2077,

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.720
which is made by CD Projekt Red, the company
behind the Witcher video games.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:17.380
The symbols that appear at the beginning of
every episode of The Witcher are full of spoilers,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.280
but you're only going to notice if you really
know your stuff.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.610
The title of each episode is always the same,
but the logo behind it changes, offering hints

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.300
as to what's about to unfold.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.360
For example, the first episode, "The End's
Beginning," features the image of a solar

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.300
eclipse.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:35.440
If you've read the short story "Lesser Evil,"
you know that the princess Renfri was born

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.620
under a so-called "black sun," leading a wizard
to assume that she was cursed.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.480
Sure enough, "The End's Beginning" tells Renfri's
story all the way to its brutal, bloody ending.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.340
"Betrayer Moon" comes with a picture of claws
and a Temerian lily, hinting at the cursed striga.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.060
The logo for "Of Banquets, Bastards and Burials"
is a sword surrounded by a tree, which is

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.380
appropriate for an episode detailing the violent
history of Ciri's family tree and her time

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.380
in the forest.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.100
In The Witcher, characters don't just speak
English.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.800
Magically-inclined characters also use elvish,
or the Elder Speech, to cast spells, lift

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.860
curses, and cause mayhem and mischief.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.909
If you listen carefully, however, you might
recognize some parts of the Elder Speech vocabulary.

00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:19.310
Author Andrezej Sapkowski based the Elder
Speech on a mishmash of different languages,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.100
including Irish, Welsh, English, German, and
Latin.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.720
She's a girl...

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.120
who possesses the power of a weapon.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.000
In Brokilon, she can be molded.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.600
But with so many different sources, how does
The Witcher's take on Elder Speech sound so good?

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.460
Easy: the language was designed by a pro.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:41.159
Sapkowski didn't actually create a fully-realized
language, so Netflix had to bring in David

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:42.959
J. Peterson to fill in the blanks.

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:44.610
Does that name sound familiar?

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:45.460
It should.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:50.620
Peterson is the same guy HBO hired to create
Dothraki and Valyrian for Game of Thrones.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.160
Netflix has made it clear that The Witcher
is based on the books, not the video games,

00:02:56.169 --> 00:02:58.870
but the show does have one major gaming connection.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.749
You just have to watch the end credits to
find it.

00:03:01.749 --> 00:03:06.199
For most viewers, the names Tomek Baginski
and Platige Image one of The Witcher's executive

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.160
producers and the Polish production company
that makes the show, respectively won't mean much.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.740
Those who really love the games, however,
know that Baginski, an Oscar-nominated animator,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:20.099
directed the stunning cinematics that brought
all three of CD Projekt Red's Witcher titles

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:20.940
to life.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.831
In fact, without Platige and Baginski, The
Witcher probably wouldn't have made it to

00:03:24.831 --> 00:03:26.219
the screen at all.

00:03:26.219 --> 00:03:30.499
Baginski, who's also on Platige's supervisory
board, was the one who pitched The Witcher

00:03:30.499 --> 00:03:31.599
to Netflix.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.280
Originally, Baginski wanted to make a feature
film based on two of Sapkowski's short stories.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:40.640
Netflix helped convince him to make a TV series
instead, and brought showrunner Lauren Schmidt

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:41.799
Hissrich on board.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.280
The rest is history.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.560
The most famous image of Geralt of Rivia isn't
the monster-slayer on horseback, riding into

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.880
the wilderness to fight some nasty beast.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.519
It's not Geralt locked in a life-or-death
battle with a ferocious creature.

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:59.299
Instead, it's the witcher soaking in a wooden
bathtub, legs spread, leaving only a little

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.560
to the imagination.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.859
It's a moment from the opening scene of CD
Projekt Red's critically acclaimed video game

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:08.900
The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt, although by now
it's become much bigger.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.400
Cosplayers started recreating the iconic moment
in photographs.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.500
CD Projekt Red and Dark Horse created a statuette
commemorating the scene.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.780
While Netflix's show is based on the books,
not the games, it pays tribute to the scene

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.080
in episode five, "Bottled Appetites."

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:27.690
Shortly after he meets Yennefer, the sorceress
lures Geralt into the bath, and you can guess

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:28.980
what happens next.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.580
The Witcher's sword fights are so impressive
that it'd be easy to assume that talented

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.060
stunt doubles handled all of the heavy lifting.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:36.980
Not true.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.840
The Witcher showrunner Lauren Schmidt Hissrich
told us in an interview that star Henry Cavill

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.960
did his own stunts on the show.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.420
Every single one of them.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:45.620
According to Hissrich:

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:49.480
"There is never a point where you see someone
on screen as Geralt that's not him.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:50.740
It's always Henry."

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.940
In fact, that proclamation extends beyond
the fight scenes.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:58.220
Cavill didn't use stand-ins, even when most
of his face and body is concealed.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:02.480
If you see Geralt's hand, his boot, or his
elbow, it's Cavill on-screen.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.560
Cavill was so dedicated to the role that he
wore his costume, including his complete set

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.360
of armor, while making breakfast or taking
a nap.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.100
Most extras carried fake swords, but Cavill's
wasn't just real.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:16.440
He helped design the weapon, customizing it
as he saw fit.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.500
For all intents and purposes, Cavill is Geralt
and his performance is all the better for it.

